RIEMANN HYPOTHESIS 
— CORE STATEMENT + FUNDAMENTALS

Zeta function (Euler product)
  ζ(s) = Σ_{n=1}^∞ n^{-s}  =  Π_p (1 − p^{-s})^{-1}
  s ∈ ℂ,   s = σ + it
  domain of absolute convergence: Re(s) > 1
  meromorphic continuation to all ℂ (simple pole at s=1)

Two classes of zeros
  Trivial zeros
    s = −2, −4, −6, …          (negative even integers)
    forced by the functional equation
    Γ(s/2) π^{-s/2} ζ(s) = Γ((1-s)/2) π^{-(1-s)/2} ζ(1-s)

  Non-trivial zeros
    lie in the critical strip  0 < Re(s) < 1
    Riemann Hypothesis (1859):  all of them satisfy  Re(s) = 1/2
    (the critical line)

Consequence for primes
  If RH holds, the prime-counting function satisfies
    π(x) = li(x) + O(√x log x)
  i.e. primes cannot deviate from their expected density
  by more than the theoretically smallest possible amount.
